WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.810 --> 00:00:05.590
Restaurants make up 4% of our GDP

00:00:05.590 --> 00:00:08.579
and employ 11 million people,

00:00:08.579 --> 00:00:09.810
and 16 million people if you look

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:11.810
at the other people in the supply chain.

00:00:12.850 --> 00:00:14.080
When COVID-19 struck,

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:16.770
it affected all corners of the economy,

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:20.120
but the restaurant industry
was hit especially hard.

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:23.470
The pandemic has forced
us to move a lot faster

00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:25.723
and ahead than we
initially had planned to.

00:00:26.594 --> 00:00:29.270
[Rep. Earl Blumenauer]
Employment rate is twice

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:30.690
the national average.

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:34.340
And we're at risk of losing, permanently,

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:36.383
tens of thousands of restaurants.

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:39.170
The restaurant industry as a whole

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:42.610
has lost an estimated
$290 billion in revenue

00:00:42.610 --> 00:00:46.020
from 2020 to 2021 with approximately

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:48.650
90,000 restaurants
across the United States

00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:50.370
closing permanently.

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:51.920
As the pandemic continues,

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:54.620
restaurants are forced to
constantly adapt and evolve.

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:58.470
There's an immense amount of opportunity

00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:00.730
for collective innovation

00:01:00.730 --> 00:01:04.130
and collaboration as we
move through this phase

00:01:04.130 --> 00:01:05.730
and into a recovery phase,

00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:08.060
and I'm excited about what we can create

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:10.240
and how we can stitch this industry

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:11.620
and our communities back together

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:13.820
better than they ever were before.

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:16.370
So which restaurants will survive

00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:19.070
and what will restaurants
look like in this new world?

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:29.890
Pre pandemic 2019,

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:32.030
that has a lot of great memories.

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:35.300
We actually opened fourth quarter of 2019.

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:37.860
We had, you know, a full staff.

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:40.140
We were actually becoming like a family.

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:41.770
Getting into the flow of things.

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:44.900
During the holidays we were
extra busy because we were new.

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:46.010
We were rocking.

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:46.890
Business was good.

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:48.970
It was looking likely that, you know,

00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:50.980
I'd maybe be able to start paying myself

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:52.633
at the end of that summer.

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:55.340
At the beginning, it was
easier to have the mindset of,

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:58.100
okay, we're just getting
through this week by week,

00:01:58.100 --> 00:01:59.210
month by month.

00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:00.180
And then towards the end,

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:02.310
it started getting really tiring.

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:04.750
You know, there's only so much of, um,

00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:06.550
we're just hanging on,
hanging on that you can do.

00:02:06.550 --> 00:02:08.100
And we did. And we, I felt like

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:10.310
we did a great job through all of that,

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:12.690
but at the end I was
starting to lose sleep.

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:16.670
I was starting to be concerned
because we were so new.

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:19.080
We didn't have a lot of cash flow.

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:20.980
When March 15th came,

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:23.020
there was a lot of trepidation.

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:25.610
Just the fear of the unknown existed.

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:28.020
I was paralyzed. We were devastated.

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:30.470
No one had experienced that before.

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:33.360
That was a dire moment for
me to say that, you know,

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:35.450
I don't know when you're
going to come back to work.

00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:39.080
I had no answers, no one
provided us with guidance.

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:40.080
They really struggled

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:42.330
on trying to figure out, okay,
can we do a takeout menu?

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:43.370
How are we going to do that?

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:45.580
They were so focused on
the customer experience

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:48.550
inside the four walls of the restaurant

00:02:48.550 --> 00:02:50.210
and shifting to, okay,

00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:51.200
we need to make revenue.

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:53.210
We need to figure out a delivery platform

00:02:53.210 --> 00:02:55.060
and a takeout menu.

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:58.440
Usually the larger chains
that had invested in digital,

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:02.170
that had invested in a
mobile order and pay app,

00:03:02.170 --> 00:03:04.669
or some sort of online ordering,

00:03:04.669 --> 00:03:07.120
they were able to pivot and much quicker

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:09.600
than those that had not
invested in technology

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:10.770
and had not invested

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:12.660
in a digital customer experience

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:14.373
or an ordering platform.

00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:17.300
It was a day by day learning process.

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:19.270
It was a learning curve every single day.

00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:21.160
Unfortunately, some of
these things, you know,

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:23.610
cost money, you know, they
require financial resources

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:25.250
that we simply did not have.

00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:26.230
All of those shifts

00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:29.200
and those decisions
were made independently

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:30.940
for different restaurants.

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:34.120
And depending on where you
were on the spectrum of

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:39.110
technology, comfortability,
safety and the local guidelines.

00:03:39.110 --> 00:03:42.873
And so you had different
restaurants doing different things.

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:49.150
Even if these adaptations were applied,

00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:51.840
restaurant owners still
faced supply chain issues,

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:54.233
labor shortages, and losses of revenue.

00:03:56.150 --> 00:03:57.270
Restaurants were struggling

00:03:57.270 --> 00:03:58.600
getting certain products.

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:00.660
They were struggling
getting certain meats.

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:02.730
And then there were
certain paper products,

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:05.340
whether it be straws or paper or.

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:07.690
So that was a huge hindrance.

00:04:07.690 --> 00:04:09.720
You can't just flip a switch back on

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:10.860
and have all the products

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:14.210
that you need immediately
flowing through the supply chain.

00:04:14.210 --> 00:04:17.080
And so restaurants are
faced with increased costs

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:20.610
on products, ranging from
proteins to cooking oils,

00:04:20.610 --> 00:04:23.640
to even things as simple as a to go box.

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:25.300
We are in a massive labor shortage.

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:29.340
We have a supply chain shortage
in terms of actual goods

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:31.070
and services that we're able to procure.

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:33.450
I've had to change my menu so many times

00:04:33.450 --> 00:04:35.820
because I'm not able to procure everything

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:38.540
that's needed for a certain recipes.

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:39.970
I would say from staff,

00:04:39.970 --> 00:04:43.670
we need quality staff and we
need training for our staff.

00:04:43.670 --> 00:04:46.840
As a result, quality
does suffer on many ends

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:49.020
and the level of service you do have

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:50.303
does suffer a little bit.

00:04:52.410 --> 00:04:53.320
Help also came

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:55.230
in the form of federal assistance.

00:04:55.230 --> 00:04:57.730
The restaurant revitalization fund.

00:04:57.730 --> 00:05:00.400
The restaurant revitalization
fund provides debt-free

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:04.060
support in the amount of
annual revenue loss from 2019

00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:07.650
and 2020 with special
provisions for businesses

00:05:07.650 --> 00:05:10.393
that opened in 2019 and 2020.

00:05:11.670 --> 00:05:13.830
The restaurant revitalization fund grants

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:17.080
were awarded to businesses
whose primary purpose

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:19.800
is that they are a place
where people gather

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:21.600
to consume food or drink.

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:22.900
So your neighborhood coffee shop,

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:25.280
your favorite date night
restaurant would qualify.

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:27.160
Then they needed to demonstrate

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:29.570
that they lost revenue in 2020.

00:05:29.570 --> 00:05:32.310
And they did that by providing
things like tax returns

00:05:32.310 --> 00:05:33.747
and point of sale reports.

00:05:33.747 --> 00:05:34.780
[Rep. Earl Blumenauer] We were able

00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:39.780
to secure $28.6 billion dollars

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:44.590
to be able to help these
independent restaurants.

00:05:44.590 --> 00:05:49.230
Over a hundred thousand
grants were distributed.

00:05:49.230 --> 00:05:50.220
But passing a bill

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:51.700
and actually getting the money

00:05:51.700 --> 00:05:54.180
into the hands of struggling restaurateurs

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:55.620
are two different things.

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:58.680
As soon as the application went live,

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:01.260
I got my application in within moments,

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:03.110
and then I got an approval.

00:06:03.110 --> 00:06:07.480
And then I got a letter stating
that because of a lawsuit

00:06:07.480 --> 00:06:08.313
that had happened,

00:06:08.313 --> 00:06:11.400
the SBA was not able
to pay out those funds.

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:13.310
[Rep. Earl Blumenauer] The
need far outstripped that.

00:06:13.310 --> 00:06:18.310
The fund was basically
exhausted in just a few days.

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:19.530
That just felt

00:06:19.530 --> 00:06:21.160
like a slap across the face

00:06:21.160 --> 00:06:22.730
and wondering what's next,

00:06:22.730 --> 00:06:24.890
and how are we going to be able
to keep up with this demand

00:06:24.890 --> 00:06:27.430
and grow without cashflow?

00:06:27.430 --> 00:06:29.183
We have no cash reserves.

00:06:30.290 --> 00:06:32.320
We sound a bit like a broken record,

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:35.710
constantly calling our members
of Congress and telling them

00:06:35.710 --> 00:06:37.260
that restaurants aren't okay.

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:38.683
Despite appearances.

00:06:39.592 --> 00:06:40.870
[Rep. Earl Blumenauer] Well, bear in mind,

00:06:40.870 --> 00:06:45.090
we've been dealing with the
near collapse of the economy.

00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:46.660
There are massive needs

00:06:46.660 --> 00:06:49.284
that have developed all
across the country for health.

00:06:49.284 --> 00:06:50.840
There's a lot of demand.

00:06:50.840 --> 00:06:53.083
There's a lot of competition.

00:06:53.083 --> 00:06:56.640
There are over $60 billion
of needs that remained

00:06:56.640 --> 00:06:58.780
when the fund was exhausted.

00:06:58.780 --> 00:07:01.020
So what we're trying to
do is replenish it so

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:04.820
that everybody who was
eligible and applied

00:07:04.820 --> 00:07:06.570
would be able to be taken care of.

00:07:06.570 --> 00:07:08.260
We have a situation

00:07:08.260 --> 00:07:10.600
where restaurants are
still dealing with the up

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:11.980
and down enclosures,

00:07:11.980 --> 00:07:13.430
increased costs,

00:07:13.430 --> 00:07:15.080
hesitant consumers.

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:17.090
So they really need financial assistance

00:07:17.090 --> 00:07:18.760
to give them the foundation

00:07:18.760 --> 00:07:21.410
to be able to continue to operate

00:07:21.410 --> 00:07:24.260
and to continue to recover
as we ride out this wave.

00:07:24.260 --> 00:07:25.840
If you drive anywhere in the US

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:26.760
and you see a restaurant,

00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:29.150
there's going to be a sign
outside that says help wanted,

00:07:29.150 --> 00:07:30.740
or that they're enticing people

00:07:30.740 --> 00:07:33.480
with a $15 per hour incentive,

00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:36.530
or some, some restaurants
have gone to $17 an hour

00:07:36.530 --> 00:07:39.830
because they can't
staff their restaurants.

00:07:39.830 --> 00:07:42.483
We have one member of our coalition

00:07:42.483 --> 00:07:46.580
who has over $1.5 million
in deferred rent payments.

00:07:46.580 --> 00:07:47.720
You're not going to pay that off

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:49.363
with just one busy Saturday.

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:51.650
And at the end of the day,

00:07:51.650 --> 00:07:54.090
it's not all about the money.

00:07:54.090 --> 00:07:55.640
Health is more important than business

00:07:55.640 --> 00:07:56.700
at the end of the day.

00:07:56.700 --> 00:07:59.210
And if we need to shut down
because it's saving lives,

00:07:59.210 --> 00:08:00.100
we'll do it.

00:08:00.100 --> 00:08:01.840
That's why we stayed take out only

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:04.960
because we were too small to
space people out appropriately,

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:07.050
but that's scary.

00:08:07.050 --> 00:08:08.120
Despite the obstacles

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:09.660
and the delta variant threatening to

00:08:09.660 --> 00:08:11.500
beat back the progress they've made,

00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:13.980
some restaurant owners
remain cautiously optimistic

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:15.372
for the future.

00:08:15.372 --> 00:08:17.550
I have a lot of trepidation about the fall

00:08:17.550 --> 00:08:18.770
and winter, cause we all know

00:08:18.770 --> 00:08:20.640
that the more people come in doors,

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:22.890
the more closer contact you
become with people that,

00:08:22.890 --> 00:08:25.150
that the virus can spread faster.

00:08:25.150 --> 00:08:26.980
The pandemic happening,

00:08:26.980 --> 00:08:28.980
gave me a chance to kind of step back

00:08:28.980 --> 00:08:30.980
and reevaluate our operation.

00:08:30.980 --> 00:08:32.203
You know, the kitchen is very, very small.

00:08:32.203 --> 00:08:33.540
It's a two person kitchen.

00:08:33.540 --> 00:08:36.870
And just to kind of see how
it is from their standpoint

00:08:36.870 --> 00:08:40.503
and, you know, just find
ways to be more efficient.

00:08:40.503 --> 00:08:41.336
I don't think

00:08:41.336 --> 00:08:44.470
that the restaurants will go
to a fully automated platform.

00:08:44.470 --> 00:08:46.900
I do feel like there
will be a healthy balance

00:08:46.900 --> 00:08:48.980
as they try to figure out the labor

00:08:48.980 --> 00:08:53.483
and wage shortage between
automation and human labor.

00:08:55.370 --> 00:08:56.930
We need consumers, you know,

00:08:56.930 --> 00:08:59.270
obviously to show up and
support our small businesses,

00:08:59.270 --> 00:09:01.570
but we need patience and understanding.

00:09:01.570 --> 00:09:04.930
And if a restaurant is going
to require you to wear a mask,

00:09:04.930 --> 00:09:07.450
it's not what they want to
do, that's not fun for them.

00:09:07.450 --> 00:09:09.800
It's what they need to do to make sure

00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:12.010
that their employees aren't sick

00:09:12.010 --> 00:09:14.520
or that the economy
doesn't shut down again.

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:17.220
We do need them to continue dining out

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:18.670
in any way that they feel comfortable,

00:09:18.670 --> 00:09:20.040
whether that's sitting on a patio,

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:21.587
whether it's purchasing to go food,

00:09:21.587 --> 00:09:23.120
going inside dining,

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:25.410
which in some places is
quite safe and lovely.

00:09:25.410 --> 00:09:27.010
It's also to be patient.

00:09:27.010 --> 00:09:30.310
These restaurants may not have
your favorite dish right now

00:09:30.310 --> 00:09:32.270
because the supply chain
isn't allowing them

00:09:32.270 --> 00:09:34.410
to purchase the ingredients they need.

00:09:34.410 --> 00:09:36.590
Also, you might notice
that prices have increased

00:09:36.590 --> 00:09:40.910
and there's only so much you
can squash down a menu price,

00:09:40.910 --> 00:09:45.680
when everything that is going
into making your dinner,

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:47.270
whether that's the ingredients

00:09:47.270 --> 00:09:49.850
or the folks preparing your food,

00:09:49.850 --> 00:09:51.483
has a price.

00:09:51.483 --> 00:09:52.316
[Rep. Earl Blumenauer]
The restaurant industry

00:09:52.316 --> 00:09:53.440
has stepped up.

00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:55.580
They've provided critical service.

00:09:55.580 --> 00:09:57.470
They've been innovative.

00:09:57.470 --> 00:09:58.890
And in many cases,

00:09:58.890 --> 00:10:01.030
they have stepped up to help people

00:10:01.030 --> 00:10:06.030
who otherwise wouldn't have
access to this healthy food.

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:08.480
Restaurants are going to be front

00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:11.960
and center of any sort
of economic recovery.

00:10:11.960 --> 00:10:14.060
They deserve our support.

00:10:14.060 --> 00:10:14.980
One of the silver linings

00:10:14.980 --> 00:10:17.060
that you don't see when you're dining

00:10:17.060 --> 00:10:19.380
is that these independent restaurants

00:10:19.380 --> 00:10:21.140
across the country have
always been the places

00:10:21.140 --> 00:10:22.620
where you go when you need help,

00:10:22.620 --> 00:10:26.430
whether it's donating something
to the little league team

00:10:26.430 --> 00:10:28.426
or the school auction,

00:10:28.426 --> 00:10:30.890
or just creating an incredible environment

00:10:30.890 --> 00:10:32.670
where you can go hang
out with your girlfriends

00:10:32.670 --> 00:10:34.220
after a really bad day.

00:10:34.220 --> 00:10:35.783
They've always been giving.

00:10:35.783 --> 00:10:37.390
And this is the first time

00:10:37.390 --> 00:10:39.060
that they've really had to ask for help.

00:10:39.060 --> 00:10:42.490
And I think that this community
has found their voice,

00:10:42.490 --> 00:10:45.260
and found how powerful their voices can be

00:10:45.260 --> 00:10:46.590
in creating change,

00:10:46.590 --> 00:10:47.520
not just for them,

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:48.820
but for their communities.

